    "description": "Hemingray Glass Company\nNo. 12 Insulator\nPressed Glass, Aqua Blue, Antique/Vintage\nAuthentic Hemingray No. 12 glass insulator, a classic piece of American industrial history. Produced by the Hemingray Glass Company, this model was widely used for telephone and telegraph lines during the late 19th and early 20th centuries. It features the signature aqua blue glass and technical design elements common to the era's utility infrastructure.\nEmbossed \"HEMINGRAY NO. 12\" on the front skirt\nEmbossed \"PATENT MAY 2 1893\" on the reverse side\nSharp drip points along the bottom base edge designed to shed moisture\nTranslucent aqua blue glass with characteristic manufacturing seed bubbles and swirls\nApproximately 3.75 inches tall by 3 inches wide\nCondition:\nSignificant chip and glass loss on the bottom skirt edge as shown in photographs. Minor surface soot and mineral deposits within the interior threads and grooves. Main dome and upper wire grooves remain structurally sound. Original glass color is vibrant with no internal stress cracks.\nPlease Read:\nAll items are donated and may exhibit signs of wear, including surface dirt, discoloration, scratches, and scuffs. Glassware and dishes may display color fading, crazing, or chips. Clothing items may have loose threads or fabric pilling. The full functionality of electrical items is not guaranteed. Purses may show wear on the bottom edges, corners, and handles. Jewelry metal content and stones have not been tested unless explicitly stated, and stones have not been individually inspected. Items sold in lots have not been counted or sorted and may contain small or damaged pieces. Returns are not accepted for these items.\nPlease carefully review photos and descriptions prior to bidding. If you have any questions or uncertainties, please contact us by opening a customer service ticket.",
